          "id": "db-basics",
          "title": "Relational Databases & SQL",
          "content": "<div class=\"topic-header\">\n<div class=\"topic-label\">Database</div>\n<div class=\"topic-title\">Database Fundamentals</div>\n<div class=\"topic-subtitle\">What is a Database · RDBMS · Tables · Relational Model</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"section-header\">What is a Database?</div>\n<ul class=\"content-list\">\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> organized, structured collection of data stored electronically, managed by a DBMS (Database Management System)</li>\n<li><strong>Properties:</strong> Persistent (survives restarts) · Shareable (multi-user concurrent access) · Consistent (rules enforced) · Queryable (SQL)</li>\n<li><strong>Without DB:</strong> flat files have no concurrent access control, no query engine, no referential integrity, no transaction support — data corruption inevitable</li>\n<li><strong>Types:</strong> Relational (MySQL, PostgreSQL) · Document (MongoDB) · Key-Value (Redis) · Column (Cassandra) · Graph (Neo4j)</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"section-header\">What is RDBMS?</div>\n<ul class=\"content-list\">\n<li>Based on E.F. Codd's relational model (1970) — data in tables, relationships via shared key values (not pointers)</li>\n<li>SQL (Structured Query Language) is the standard interface — declarative (\"what\", not \"how\")</li>\n<li>Features: ACID transactions, referential integrity (FK), normalization, concurrent access via MVCC/locking</li>\n<li><strong>Java ↔ RDBMS:</strong> JDBC API → <code>DriverManager.getConnection()</code> → <code>PreparedStatement</code> → <code>ResultSet</code></li>\n<li>Modern Java: JPA/Hibernate ORM maps Java objects ↔ DB tables automatically (<code>@Entity</code>, <code>@Table</code>, <code>@Column</code>)</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"section-header\">What are Tables?</div>\n<ul class=\"content-list\">\n<li>A table (relation) = rows (tuples) × columns (attributes). Each row = one entity instance. Each column = one attribute with a defined domain (data type)</li>\n<li>Row order is not significant — a table is a mathematical set, not a list</li>\n<li>Each cell must be atomic (First Normal Form) — no arrays or sub-tables in a cell</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" style=\"margin-top:1rem\">\n<table>\n<tr><th colspan=\"5\" style=\"text-align:center;color:#4f9eff\">Students Table</th></tr>\n<tr><th>StudentID (PK)</th><th>Name</th><th>Email</th><th>DeptID (FK)</th><th>CGPA</th></tr>\n<tr><td>101</td><td>Alice</td><td>alice@uni.edu</td><td>CS</td><td>8.9</td></tr>\n<tr><td>102</td><td>Bob</td><td>bob@uni.edu</td><td>EE</td><td>7.5</td></tr>\n<tr><td>103</td><td>Carol</td><td>carol@uni.edu</td><td>CS</td><td>9.1</td></tr>\n</table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"imported-theory\"><div class=\"section-header\">Theoretical Content Details</div><div class=\"section-header\">What is a Database?</div><ul class=\"content-list\">\n<li>An organized, structured collection of data stored electronically and managed by a DBMS</li>\n<li>Data is stored persistently — survives program restarts and system failures</li>\n<li>Designed to be queried, updated, and managed efficiently</li>\n<li>Properties: structured (schema-defined), persistent, consistent, shareable (multi-user)</li>\n<li>Without a database: applications store data in flat files — no concurrent access control, no query capability, no referential integrity</li>\n</ul><div class=\"section-header\">What is RDBMS?</div><ul class=\"content-list\">\n<li>Relational Database Management System — software that manages a relational database</li>\n<li>Based on E.F. Codd's relational model (1970): data stored in tables (relations); relationships expressed via shared data values (keys), not pointers</li>\n<li>Examples: MySQL, PostgreSQL, Oracle, SQL Server, SQLite</li>\n<li>Key features: SQL interface, ACID transactions, referential integrity (foreign keys), normalization support, concurrent access control</li>\n<li>Java connection: JDBC (Java Database Connectivity) is the API that lets Java programs communicate with any RDBMS</li>\n</ul><div class=\"section-header\">What are Tables?</div><ul class=\"content-list\">\n<li>A table (relation) is a 2-dimensional structure: rows (tuples/records) × columns (attributes/fields)</li>\n<li>Each row represents one entity instance (one student, one order, one product)</li>\n<li>Each column represents one attribute (studentID, name, age, email)</li>\n<li>All values in a column must be of the same data type (domain)</li>\n<li>Order of rows is not meaningful — a table is a set, not a list</li>\n<li>Each cell should contain an atomic value (no repeating groups — 1NF requirement)</li>\n</ul></div>\n<!-- ============================================================ -->\n<!-- DATABASE: KEYS -->\n<!-- ============================================================ -->"
